          "worked": "If $\\cos\\alpha=0$, then $\\sin^2\\alpha=1$ and the given left-hand side is $\\frac12$, contradicting $\\frac15$. Thus division by $\\cos^4\\alpha$ is valid.\n\nLet $t=\\tan^2\\alpha$. Dividing the given equation by $\\cos^4\\alpha$ and using $\\sec^2\\alpha=1+\\tan^2\\alpha$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{\\tan^4\\alpha}{2}+\\frac13&=\\frac{\\sec^4\\alpha}{5}\\\\\n\\frac{t^2}{2}+\\frac13&=\\frac{(1+t)^2}{5}\\\\\n15t^2+10&=6(1+2t+t^2)\\\\\n9t^2-12t+4&=0\\\\\n(3t-2)^2&=0\\\\\nt&=\\frac23\\\\\n\\tan^2\\alpha&=\\frac23.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
